Apple external display host regressions
======================================

Run from a kernel source checkout:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=drivers/gpu/apple-dcp run_tests

Or keep translation units, commands, failure logs and source hashes:

  tools/testing/selftests/drivers/gpu/apple-dcp/run.sh --output /tmp/apple-dcp-results

An installed copy needs --source /path/to/kernel. --group edid, rate, phy or
lifecycle or boot selects one group. HOSTCC selects the host compiler (default clang).
Python 3 and a compiler supporting AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
are required. The usb-kconfig producer also needs GNU Make 4 or newer. The
runner uses MAKE when set, otherwise gmake if available, then make. On macOS,
install GNU Make or select an existing producer with KCONFIG_CONF. The host
fixtures use native errno definitions; Linux-only reply statuses stay local
to the synthetic callback test.

No target access, firmware command, captured fixture or kernel
configuration is required.

One TAP entry point uses shared production extraction and compiler handling.
The modules contain public synthetic host boundaries and retained assertions:

* edid: actual AFK admission and OPEN/CLOSE handling; native/HDMI providers
  alternating over 64 epochs; foreign owners, stale replies, reader/command
  pins, ordered receive progress and legacy service behavior.
* rate: actual binary parser, EDID/capacity policy, receipt ingress, getters
  and lookup; all arrival orders, conflicting/discarded siblings, rejected
  replacement catalogs, HPD retirement and recovery; malformed/duplicate
  metadata and independent EDID decoding; eight bounded delayed retries.
* phy: actual parking/mux/init/reset/configure callbacks, masked PMA context,
  faulted ACK/BIST cleanup, dirty-route retry, OFF/new-calibration epochs,
  warm reset and legacy T8103/T8122/T8140/T8142 route controls.
* lifecycle: actual physical-disconnect authority and ownership despite failed
  teardown; a fast dock replug whose DPTX link never configures (withdrawn,
  unacknowledged, unanswered, refused and live failures); an old disconnect's
  delayed CRTC power-off racing a replacement M1/M2 Type-C session; a dock
  that leaves during the first mode set, whose dropped present keeps the IOMFB
  queue busy (deferred and retried stop, never-retired, post-power-off and
  crashed-queue latches); DWC3/core/PHY PM
  and reset-reference recovery; exact firmware
  tuple/resource admission, unsupported VRR rejection and power reply bounds;
  15,552 D134 dispatcher scope/length/context and legacy reply cases.

Transport, MMIO, kernel locks and work scheduling are host mocks. These tests
establish source behavior; electrical readiness, firmware/FDT ownership,
concurrency and system suspend require separate kernel/hardware qualification.
Kernel KUnit tests and apple-afkv2 remain separate tests in their existing
standard layouts. No archived diagnostic script is a dependency of this suite.

The display group's desktop-gamma fixture executes the production A420/A421
callbacks and composition with synthetic tables. It checks full return-status
handling, the external empty status-2 reply, internal-panel diagnostics,
malformed tables, independent RGB interpolation and restoration of the base.
It does not establish firmware table encoding or external A421 support.

The runner preserves host artifacts on failure as well as success. Existing
physical-disconnect fixture warnings for unused mock boundaries and signed
host/kernel ABI comparisons are handled only by those fixtures' original
compiler options; production source compilation uses the normal Kbuild policy.

The bounded-PM group shares the PHY fixture and executes the actual PM get/put
and mux wrapper, usb_hc_died and typec_mux_set. The probe group executes real
CD321x register/unregister/rollback and retry-state functions. USB n/y/m uses
the actual optional dependency and a real Kconfig producer (KCONFIG_CONF may
select an existing producer). Legacy lane/teardown groups keep executing the
same locked mux body; they do not pretend their old USB mocks own runtime PM.
The cpuidle group executes the actual Apple table/probe, generic registration
and s2idle selector/entry, and tick_freeze/unfreeze. It checks source behavior
only; RTC, elapsed suspend time and simultaneous hardware execution remain
separate qualification gates. The original human cpuidle patch is preserved.

The service-rebind group also runs retained reviewer controls through the
actual sender, receive worker, recovery, quiesce and EDID wrapper. It covers
CLOSED cancellation before lookup, first late ACK versus already consumed
duplicates, reader-pinned cross-slot/channel reuse, and the explicit physical
absence window. Per-interface sequence history has a fixed 64-KiB bound per
AFKV2 endpoint; reply routing still ignores reader-only retired services.

Two wrap controls intentionally retain the command-17 replay assertion
failure, for OOB and inline. The group verifies that these counterexamples
still reproduce, alongside nonvacuous mutations and passing corrected safety
controls. A passing host suite does not establish a replay-exclusion contract
or admit a hardware trial. Token/sequence semantics remain a separate protocol
fact to establish; the tests do not manufacture a firmware echo guarantee.

The boot group executes the actual OSLOG dispatcher with synthetic fixed-width
words. Opaque event types 2-5 use debug logging, unknown types retain warnings,
and only the existing buffer-request path can allocate/ACK buffers. The EDID
service-rebind group also distinguishes intentional optional EOPNOTSUPP from
required admission failures and genuine optional-service errors, and proves
refused services never OPEN or publish. No captured firmware values are used.

The ATC lane/PIPE and DWC3 teardown fixtures use a shared host adapter around
both the extracted Type-C target-mode mapper and the extracted locked mux body.
They omit USB PM at that boundary. The PM fixtures replace the adapter with the
actual mux update and callback, including the scoped no-op check: a clean
same-mode request needs neither root-hub lookup nor PM, while dirty lanes,
pending PIPE work and a mismatched PIPE still require ownership. KUnit static
redirects are disabled in host fixtures so the production bodies execute.
CD321x probe uses the actual optional-router lookup with a modeled deferred
probe decision; both supplier waiting and optional-router fallback are checked.

With the fixed-hub SuperSpeed handover the locked mux body is
atcphy_mux_switch(), and the PM fixtures run the actual USB side around it:
the SuperSpeed root port goes down before the PIPE moves, the device on it
with it, and comes up only once the PIPE is back on the USB3 backend of the
running host. A modeled port refuses PIPE moves while it is up, and its
power-off and power-on failures, a port someone else switched off and the
retries the update queues are checked. The adapter of the lane fixtures takes
no port down and checks that none comes up. A failed USB3 power-off or reset
leaves dwc3 out of host mode, and a fixed-hub host whose PIPE cannot be routed
still starts. Each fixture detects these from the source, so the expectations
for sources without the handover remain.

The standalone plane-layout.py test executes the production atomic check and
update, the DRM block-format descriptors, and the surface structs with synthetic
GEM sizes. It checks RGB8/RGB10/FP16 and semiplanar YUV layouts, tile padding,
metadata, offsets, GEM boundaries, arithmetic overflow and invisible planes.
Packed P030/P230 remain supported for LINEAR; INTERCHANGE is refused and omitted
from per-format modifier advertisement until its packed tile layout is known.
DRM's three-pixel block descriptor establishes linear storage, not how those
blocks cross the interchange tile and compression-subtile boundaries.
Run `./plane-layout.py --mutations --ref <old-public-revision> --output <dir>`
to retain the compiled before/after controls, baseline reproducer and runtime
mutation results. A compile failure never counts as a mutation kill. DRM
clipping, GEM/DMA allocation and firmware execution are outside this host test.

The m4 group executes the actual M4 firmware codecs against ten immutable
A407 request fixtures generated from the working kernel at
feb6464a52a7d968d8e7a0ac6a5cab2865f31782. All 71,280 bytes are compared,
including XRGB/ARGB, cursor updates/removal, blanking, and 0/120/500-nit
brightness. The fixture records original source hashes and synthetic input
source; running the tests never regenerates expected bytes from current code.
Logical blanking deliberately replaces the former purple diagnostic background
with opaque black; only the four-byte background field of the two blanking
fixtures permits that documented change. Protocol tests also cover independent
A407/D590 receipt ordering, exact swap
IDs, malformed/duplicate replies, READY identity, packet bounds, nested
callback routing, DMA aperture/extent bounds and cursor admission. These
remain CPU regressions, not firmware or display qualification. The common
plane callback runs against synthetic DRM-core boundaries to check strict
linear DMA GEM admission and active/invisible rejection. Real prepare_fb
keeps producer preparation while the backend owns v26_6 framebuffer retirement;
legacy retirement remains tested. Actual backend present/blank functions and
the real common backlight state/wrappers run with a bounded mock transport,
covering DPMS-off before delayed work and retaining active plus uncertain
framebuffers when one of the two required receipts never arrives.

run-native-m4-kms.py defaults to dry run; --compile-only needs libdrm development
headers and never opens DRM. Explicit --execute requires root, an exact fresh
source/Image/boot identity, the common appledrm module, a stopped display
manager and the independent unqualified-candidate rollback guard. It resolves
the internal CRTC through the eDP encoder mask, performs 60 bounded flips, a
CRTC-only atomic commit, then 20 blank/unblank cycles. The operator owns desktop stop
and restart plus kernel health monitoring; the runner never disarms rollback.
This hardware runner is excluded from ordinary selftest execution.

M4 error-path regression checks can also run independently:

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/drivers/gpu/apple-dcp/m4-error-paths.py

They compile the actual PMP vote state transitions, borrowed-DART fault
handler, retained-resource cleanup action and failed-flip event handoff with
host mocks under UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. Controls cover accepted DISP
surviving an unrelated failed AGX request, both reply/timeout lock orderings,
malformed replies, true owner failure, an unowned or invalid fault SID, probe
rollback versus published DMA lifetime, exactly-once DRM event ownership, and
mixed-owner, truncated, failed-translation and invalid-owner IOMMU tuples.

Internal panel policy
---------------------
The display group covers the shared nits ramp on a 600-nit panel, retargeting,
blanking, suspend, rejected submits and both submit/completion callback orders.
Brightness-only commits retain the active gamma state without marking it changed.
Synthetic TimingElements exercise 120 Hz and fractional lower refresh rates,
DCP color/timing identities, invalid geometry and adaptive-sync ranges. The
adaptive-sync callback test verifies the desktop parameter record, mode-info
ordering and one fixed-refresh retry after a rejected enable or modeset. The
watchdog test checks fixed-refresh recovery, retained-buffer arming and late
completion isolation.
These tests do not establish which timings a particular panel reports or prove
refresh rate, flicker, brightness smoothness or adaptive-sync on hardware.

Desktop panel brightness regressions also cover panel EDID parsing and
publication from ProductAttributes without optional color data. Both bare and
descriptor-wrapped powerlog reports pass through the production compact receiver,
systemep handler and mNits parser. Rapid key repeats with interleaved stale and
new reports must converge monotonically to the last user target in both
directions. Report observations cannot restart a fade or request another write.
The callback cases exercise the desktop D202/D454 wire adapters, physical resource
mapping and release, and unknown-callback failure followed by a successful set.

Panel identity tests also retain the EDID and generation across repeated and
partial dictionaries, and notify only when valid identity data actually changes.

The identity case also exercises the real D552/D561 table bindings, nested
DisplayAttributes and direct ProductAttributes, legacy manufacturer codes,
minimal identity data, and unchanged byte/padded boolean acknowledgments.

Desktop identity diagnostics cover bounded traversal of key names and types,
including truncated and oversized dictionaries. ADT identity tests use only
synthetic nodes and panel serials, reject truncated trees transactionally and
ensure the machine's serial cannot override the panel's. Panel EDID tests cover
a missing firmware identity, real EDID priority and invalid replacement retention.
Desktop backlight notification tests exercise accepted, rejected, duplicate and
out-of-order completions and preserve the requested target during notification.

The audio group executes the native AV call and OSObject reply boundaries with
synthetic transport replies, including truncated bodies, inconsistent used
sizes and transport errors. It retains the legacy reply behavior. KUnit's
apple-av-native-audio suite separately runs service replacement, Terminate and
link lifecycle checks for both external 14.7 and desktop 26.x firmware roles.
These fixtures do not establish the desktop audio firmware ABI or SIO support.
